    "result_text": "The full suite is just `AcceptanceTest` (2 tests) \u2014 both green, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented postcode-preferred locality derivation:\n\n**`CityRegionResolver.java`** \u2014 added an overload `regionFor(String city, String postcode)` that looks up the region by postcode range first, falling back to the city table:\n- New private `regionForPostcode(postcode)` scans the existing `REGION_POSTCODE_RANGE` table (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099) and returns the matching region, or `null` when the postcode is absent, blank, non-numeric, or in no known range.\n- `regionFor(city, postcode)` returns the postcode-derived region when present, otherwise delegates to the existing `regionFor(city)` (which returns `UNKNOWN` for unlisted cities).\n- The original single-arg `regionFor` and `isPostcodeValidForCity` are untouched, so validation behavior is unchanged.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `locality(owner)` now calls `regionFor(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode())` instead of city-only.\n\nThis returns the same region for known cities with matching postcodes (e.g. Sydney/2000 \u2192 NSW) while disambiguating via postcode \u2014 an unknown city with postcode 3000 now resolves to VIC, exactly as the two new acceptance tests assert.",
